fresh_table=table('fresh_results',['Pipeline / role','Accuracy','95% accuracy CI','Macro F1'],[[l, f"{100*M[n]['accuracy']:.2f}%",ci(n),f"{M[n]['macro_f1']:.4f}"]for n,l in zip(names,labels)])
page('Abstract',P('A completed study of seven visual material categories | 27 October 2026'),
 P('This project evaluates material classification on synthetic Blender renders while separating source families, model selection and final assessment. The development corpus contains 3,150 images from 105 material IDs and 101 known source families. A newly collected holdout contains 630 images from 21 additional source groups, with 90 images and three groups per class.'),
 P('The primary model, a frozen multiscale DINOv2 and ConvNeXt representation with a logistic classifier, was selected using development data and locked before holdout prediction. It achieved 80.16% accuracy (95% source-group bootstrap CI 66.03-93.65%) and Macro F1 0.7952. The 80% point-accuracy target was met; the lower confidence bound did not meet 80%.'),
 P('Fine-tuned DINOv2 achieved the highest observed comparison accuracy, 86.83% (95% CI 76.98-95.87%), with Macro F1 0.8631. It remains an exploratory comparison, not a retrospectively substituted primary. Its paired accuracy advantage was 6.67 percentage points (95% CI -2.06 to 16.19), which does not establish superiority.'),
 P('Five image corruptions expose a major compression limitation: primary accuracy falls to 59.05% at JPEG quality 30. Warm batch-one inference took a median 42.876 ms on the measured CUDA system. These results support controlled synthetic-render classification, with uncertainty from few source families and no validation on arbitrary real-world photographs.'),
 T(fresh_table,'Clean holdout: the same 630 images for all four locked pipelines. Detailed results and sources appear in Sections 9-12 and Appendix A.'),
 P('The report, raw prediction evidence, actual training histories, dataset source audit and complete local study package are available for reproduction. No model fitting or inference was performed to produce this report.'))

page('1. Research question and related work',
 P('Can a model identify wood, metal, stone, concrete, ceramic, fabric and leather on renders of previously unseen source families? The practical target is at least 80% image-level accuracy. The scientific question is narrower than physical material identification: the labels describe the visible appearance of archived PBR assets in a controlled scene.'),
 P('Rendering permits repeatable camera and lighting variation while holding scene geometry constant. Thirty views of one texture, however, remain correlated. A convincing evaluation must separate source families rather than randomly splitting images. This study therefore records both image counts and the smaller number of known family groups.'),
 H('Representations and evaluation design'),
 P('DINOv2 learns transferable visual representations through self-supervised pretraining [1]. Register tokens support internal transformer computation [2]; this implementation excludes them from image-patch pooling. ConvNeXt provides a modern convolutional architecture [3], while ResNet supplies a residual-network baseline [4]. These references motivate the pipeline choices but do not establish performance on this corpus.'),
 P('The frozen approach combines global CLS tokens with patch mean and population standard deviation at two resolutions. This captures both overall appearance and variation in local features. Combining transformer and convolutional representations is tested as a pipeline choice, not claimed as a new backbone architecture.'),
 P('Selecting hyperparameters and estimating performance on the same samples can create selection bias [5]. Five outer source-group folds assess the frozen selection procedure; three inner folds choose its representation and regularization. A later, independently collected holdout assesses the explicitly locked final models.'),
 H('Contribution and scope'),
 P('The contribution is an auditable small-data workflow: source provenance, grouped selection, a fixed comparison baseline, a disclosed exploratory adaptation, one locked clean holdout assessment, corruption diagnostics and measured deployment costs. Historical results and unsuccessful conditions remain visible. Pretraining, resolution and adaptation budgets differ across pipelines, so this is not a controlled causal test of architecture alone.'))

